<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayezid II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1447">1447</a>–<a href="/years/1512">1512</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Bayezid II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayezid II was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1481 to 1512, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. His early reign was shaped by the rivalry of his brother Cem. He welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1492, took Kilia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Akkerman</a> from Moldavia in 1484, and in the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of 1499–1503 captured Lepanto, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a>. He was forced to abdicate by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> in 1512.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii.jpg" alt="Bayezid II" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Veronese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paolo_Veronese_(Nachfolger)_-_Sultan_Bajozeth_II._-_2246_-_Bavarian_State_Painting_Collections.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1484 <b>Sultan Bayezid II</b> captured Chilia and Cetatea Albă, cutting Moldavia off from the sea, and in 1497 Stephen beat a Polish invasion under John I Albert in the Cosmin Forest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottoman mosques emulated its style a thousand years later: the Şehzade Mosque, the Süleymaniye Mosque, the Rüstem Pasha Mosque and the <b>Bayezid II</b> Mosque among them.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Only a son of the dynasty could reign, and the throne went to the prince who prevailed over his brothers, sometimes before his father’s death: Selim I forced his father <b>Bayezid II</b> to abdicate in 1512.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1500, during the Ottoman-Venetian war, <b>Sultan Bayezid II</b> stormed Modon after a long siege, and Coron surrendered soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-modon-and-coron">Venetian Modon and Coron</a></li></ul></section>
